      "text": "Let's assume that Jane's current age is $x$ years old.\nOne year ago, Jane was $x-1$ years old, and since it was a perfect square, we can write $x-1=n^{2}$ for some integer $n$.\nOne year from now, Jane will be $x+1$ years old, and since it will be a perfect cube, we can write $x+1=k^{3}$ for some integer $k$.\nWe also know that one year ago, the age $x-1$ and the age $x+1$ are consecutive perfect squares and perfect cubes, respectively, so we can write $x-1$ and $x+1$ as the difference between two consecutive perfect squares and perfect cubes, respectively. \nThus, we have $\\sqrt{x-1}-\\sqrt{x+1} = 2$ and $\\sqrt{x+1} -\\sqrt{x-1} = 2$.\nThese two equations give us $\\sqrt{x-1}\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{x-1}\\sqrt{x+1}-\\sqrt{x+1}\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{x+1}\\sqrt{x-1} = 4$.\nSimplifying, we get $2\\sqrt{(x-1)(x)}-2(x-1)=4$, $2(x+1)-4(x+1)+2(x-1)=4$, and $4(x-1)+2(x+1)-2(x+1)(x-1)=4$. \nSubstituting $x-1=n^{2}$ and $x+1=k^{3}$ into the equations, we have $2\\sqrt{n^{2}k^{6}}-4nk^{3}+2nk^{3}+8=4$.\nThis gives us $2nk^{6}-2nk^{3}+8=4$, $nk^{6}-nk^{3}+4=2$, $n^{2}k^{6}-n^{2}k^{3}+2n^{2}=2$.\nWe can divide the first equation by 2 and the second equation by 2 to get $nk^{6}-nk^{3}+4=2$, and $n^{2}k^{6}-n^{2}k^{3}+2n^{2}=2$.\nThis means that $x-1=n^{2}$ and $x+1=2(k^{3})^{3}$.\nSubstituting these into the equation $x+1=2k^{9}$, we get $2(k^{3})^{3}=2(k^{3})^{9}$.\nThus, we have $k^{3}=k^{9}$, $k^{3}-k^{9}=0$, and $k^{2}+k^{6}=0$. \nFrom the equation $k^{2}+k^{6}=0$, we can infer that $k^{2}=-k^{6}$ and $k^{6}=-k^{2}$. \nThis means that $k^{2}$ and $k^{6}$ are both negative, so $k$ must be a negative integer.\nSince $k^{3}=k^{9}$, we can infer that $k^{6}=k^{9}$, so $k^{3}=k^{6}$.\nThus, we have $k^{3}=k$, $k=1$, and $n=1$.\nSince $x-1=n^{2}$, we have $x-1=1$, so $x=2$.\nThus, Jane's current age is $\\boxed{2}$ years old.",
